5. DOMAIN Q&A — procurement knowledge
======================================================================
[6.8s]
A Purchase Order (PO) is a formal document issued by a buyer to a supplier, authorizing the purchase of goods or services at specified prices and quantities, serving as a binding commitment; an Invoice is a bill issued by the supplier after delivery, requesting payment for the goods or services provided. Matching them in a 3-way match—comparing the PO, the receiving report (or goods receipt), and the invoice—ensures that the buyer only pays for what was ordered, received, and at the agreed-upon price, preventing overpayment, fraud, and discrepancies, thereby maintaining financial accuracy and operational integrity in procurement.

